I know there are other ways I could do this, but I'm really trying to become better with PowerShell, and I cannot for the life of me figure out why this isn't working. 
 I have a directory of HTML files, and I'm iterating over them with PowerShell, sending them to wkhtmltopdf, and trying to output them to a different directory as PDFs. Here's the command I'm trying to use: 
 get-childitem -r -attributes !directory -path test_input | % {$_.fullname} | foreach-object { & "wkhtmltopdf.exe" -O landscape --enable-local-file-access $_ "test_output\\$_.pdf" } 
 I suspect the problem is the \\$_.pdf part because I have messed with that part a bunch, and if I do this instead of the above: 
 get-childitem -r -attributes !directory -path test_input | % {$_.fullname} | foreach-object { & "wkhtmltopdf.exe" -O landscape --enable-local-file-access $_ "test_output\\$($_.length).pdf" } 
 It works, except the name of the PDF will be what is presumably the length of the full filepath of the original file, so it'll be "90.pdf." 
 If, instead of length I use namestring: 
 get-childitem -r -attributes !directory -path test_input | % {$_.fullname} | foreach-object { & "wkhtmltopdf.exe" -O landscape --enable-local-file-access $_ "test_output\\$($_.namestring).pdf" } 
 The name of the pdf will just be ".pdf" - it seems to be completely ignoring the variable in that case. I've tried a bunch of different properties on the $_ object, and length is the only one that has substituted a value, so I have no idea what's going on.

